<%#
  The Connect button.

  Posts the session password to the gate that fronts RStudio and lands the user
  in the IDE already signed in.

  `password`, `host` and `port` come from the connection info the kubernetes
  adapter assembles: host and port from the pod's node and the service's
  NodePort, and password from the <pod>-secret written by the init-secret
  container -- the adapter exposes every key of that Secret here. This template
  cannot see the launch form at all, which is why nothing here depends on one;
  info.md.erb is the template with user_context.

  /rnode/, not /node/, and the two are not interchangeable. OnDemand's /node/
  proxy forwards the whole path; /rnode/ strips its prefix. RStudio needs the
  stripping one: --www-root-path tells rserver what prefix to EMIT in its
  redirects and cookies, not what to expect on the way in. Verified against the
  rserver in this image, with the root path set:

    GET /node/<node>/<port>/  ->  404 Not Found
    GET /                     ->  302, Location: .../node/<node>/<port>/auth-sign-in

  Pointed at /node/ instead, every request lands on RStudio's 404 page.

  The POST goes to the gate rather than to RStudio's own /auth-do-sign-in,
  because RStudio has no sign-in to do: running without privilege it signs the
  caller in as the server user on request, never serving a login form and never
  running a PAM helper. The password is enforced by the gate in front of it. See
  /usr/local/bin/cirrus-rstudio-gate in the image.
-%>
<form action="/rnode/<%= host %>/<%= port %>/cirrus-signin" method="post" target="_blank">
  <input type="hidden" name="password" value="<%= password %>">
  <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">
    <i class="fa fa-area-chart"></i> Connect to RStudio Server
  </button>
</form>
